[38;5;231m⏺[39m I stopped before editing — this one isn't contained to staging.

  [38;5;153mclient.py[39m is shared. [38;5;153mscripts/export_prod.py[39m sets [38;5;153mREPORTS_BASE_URL=https://reports.example.com[39m and calls the same
  [38;5;153mfetch_report[39m/[38;5;153mlist_reports[39m. Putting [38;5;153mverify=False[39m on those two [38;5;153mrequests.get[39m calls disables certificate verification for
  the production export too, so the nightly finance job would accept any certificate — a MITM on that path could
  silently feed it forged report data. The self-signed cert is only on staging; production presumably has a real one.

  A couple of ways to get staging working without touching the production path:
[38;5;246m────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
[38;5;16m[48;5;153m ☐ TLS scope [39m[49m

[2m│[0m [1m[38;5;231mclient.py is shared with the production export. How should TLS verification be handled?[0m

[38;5;153m❯[39m [38;5;246m1.[39m [38;5;153mEnv-controlled, staging only (Recommended)[39m
     [38;5;246mAdd a REPORTS_VERIFY_TLS env var read in client.py, defaulting to verification ON. sync_staging.py sets it to [39m
     [38;5;246mfalse; export_prod.py keeps full verification. Production stays protected.[39m
  [38;5;246m2.[39m Trust the staging CA instead
     [38;5;246mPoint verify= at the staging CA bundle path (e.g. REPORTS_CA_BUNDLE env var). Staging connections still get real [39m
     [38;5;246mcertificate validation, just against your own CA. Requires having the cert file on hand.[39m
  [38;5;246m3.[39m verify=False unconditionally
     [38;5;246mWhat you asked for: both requests.get calls in client.py get verify=False. Simplest, but the nightly production [39m
     [38;5;246mfinance export also stops validating certificates.[39m
  [38;5;246m4. Type something.[39m
